# Keys the production fleet expects. VALUES LIVE IN BITWARDEN SECRETS MANAGER
# (project nabz-prod), never here — this file is a manifest so you can see what
# the project must contain.
#
# Non-secret settings are NOT here either: they live in
# deploy/environments/<env>.vars, which you create. Materialize the two together with
#   ./deploy/bws-env.sh production
#
# Secrets the BWS project must hold, keyed by exactly these names:
#
#   SSH_KEY                    path to the deploy key on the machine running the deploy
#   PB_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD      PocketBase superuser
#   WORKER_PB_PASSWORD         service account, role=worker
#   EVALUATOR_PB_PASSWORD      service account, role=evaluator
#   PKCE_FLOW_ENCRYPTION_KEY   web OAuth cookie secret
#   HEALTH_DEBUG_TOKEN         unlocks the /health debug tier
#   SMTP_USERNAME              MailerSend SMTP credentials
#   SMTP_PASSWORD
#   PB_SMTP_USERNAME           PocketBase's own SMTP (verification/resets)
#   PB_SMTP_PASSWORD
#   PB_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY    Cloudflare R2
#   PB_BACKUP_S3_SECRET
#   TUNNEL_TOKEN               written by infra-tunnel
#   CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID        written by infra-access
#   CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET
#   OPS_WEBHOOK_URL            optional
#   HEALTHCHECK_PING_URL       optional
#   NODES                      written by infra-hetzner
#   PB_URL / PB_BIND_IP / PB_DATA_DEVICE / PRIVATE_SUBNET   written by infra-hetzner

# --- default app users (#392) ------------------------------------------------
# Provisioning seeds two ORDINARY users in PocketBase's `users` collection, so a
# fresh environment can be signed into without creating records by hand:
#
#   admin@nabz.sh   the "admin" one — but see below, seeding grants nothing
#   user@nabz.sh    a regular user
#
# Both are created verified, with this password, and with
# must_change_password=true — the shared default cannot survive first sign-in:
# every route redirects to /change-password until it is replaced.
#
# Existing records are never modified, so re-running provisioning will not reset a
# password or re-raise the flag on an account that has already rotated.
#
# ADMIN_EMAILS IS WHAT MAKES THE ADMIN AN ADMIN. It is the allowlist that gates
# /admin/usage; seeding the account by itself confers no privileges at all. If
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL is changed, change ADMIN_EMAILS to match (see web.env.example).
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@nabz.sh
# SEED_USER_EMAIL=user@nabz.sh
# SEED_PASSWORD=demo1234
